shairport-sync-ssrc0-fallback.patch: let shairport-sync play Music Assistant's buffered AirPlay 2 stream.

Music Assistant's AirPlay 2 sender (cliairplay) sends SSRC 0 on every buffered audio packet, where an
Apple sender names the packet's format there (ALAC 48 kHz/24-bit, AAC and so on). shairport-sync takes
SSRC 0 for a filler block and plays silence. cliairplay also numbers its packets with a 16-bit RTP
sequence number (the payload type, 103, is in the byte above it) and sends 16-bit flushUntilSeq values,
where shairport-sync compares 23-bit ones, so the flush on a track change never finishes.

While a session has never sent a nonzero SSRC, this patch:
  - takes the packet format from the audioFormat SETUP announced (ssrc_from_audio_format),
  - compares sequence numbers and flush points in 16 bits (seq_diff),
  - and adds ALAC 44.1 kHz/24-bit (ALAC_44100_S24_2, a made-up SSRC like ALAC_44100_S16_2), which
    Music Assistant sends and Apple senders don't.
The first nonzero SSRC turns all of it off for the rest of the session (a TECHO5 change to the original),
so an Apple sender that opens with an SSRC 0 filler block is handled exactly as without the patch.

Written by iVolt1 (https://github.com/iVolt1) in https://github.com/HuskerMinion/techo5/issues/468.
Upstream, https://github.com/mikebrady/shairport-sync/issues/2319, leaves it to the sender to fix, so
TECHO5 carries it. Drop this patch once Music Assistant's sender sends real SSRCs and 23-bit sequence
numbers. Applied after shairport-sync-stdout-delay.patch, against shairport-sync 5.6.3.

--- a/ap2_buffered_audio_processor.c
+++ b/ap2_buffered_audio_processor.c
@@ -99,6 +99,16 @@
   debug(2, "Connection %d: rtp_buffered_audio_processor exit.", conn->connection_number);
